          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 51-year-old perimenopausal woman presents with irregular heavy bleeding for 3 months. After a negative pregnancy test, which investigation should take priority?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum TSH and prolactin levels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While thyroid and prolactin disorders can cause AUB-O, they are secondary concerns in a perimenopausal woman. The priority is excluding endometrial malignancy through tissue sampling.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Endometrial sampling</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In perimenopausal and postmenopausal women, excluding malignancy (AUB-M) takes precedence over all other investigations, and endometrial sampling must not be delayed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Day-21 serum progesterone to confirm ovulation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Confirming anovulation is useful in reproductive-age women, but is not the priority here. In the perimenopausal age group, excluding endometrial malignancy must come first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coagulation screen for von Willebrand disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Coagulopathy (AUB-C) is an important consideration, especially in younger women with heavy periods since menarche. In a 51-year-old with new-onset irregular bleeding, endometrial malignancy must be excluded first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In perimenopausal and postmenopausal women with AUB, the investigative priority is excluding malignancy (AUB-M). Endometrial sampling must be performed without delay, regardless of other suspected causes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. In the perimenopausal and postmenopausal age group, excluding endometrial malignancy is the single most important priority — endometrial sampling must not be delayed."
                }
              ],
