          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with PCOS presents with irregular heavy periods. Ultrasound </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with PCOS presents with irregular heavy periods. Ultrasound reveals a 3 cm submucosal fibroid. Which FIGO AUB categories best classify her bleeding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>AUB-L only, since the submucosal fibroid is the structural cause</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The fibroid (leiomyoma) qualifies as AUB-L, but ignoring her PCOS-related ovulatory dysfunction misses a concurrent non-structural cause that is also contributing to her abnormal bleeding.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>AUB-O only, since PCOS is the underlying primary disorder</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PCOS does cause ovulatory dysfunction (AUB-O), but a submucosal fibroid is an independent structural cause of heavy bleeding that must also be classified — real patients frequently have concurrent categories.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>AUB-A and AUB-E</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>There is no mention of adenomyosis (AUB-A) on imaging, and endometrial haemostatic dysfunction (AUB-E) is a diagnosis of exclusion. The clinical picture points to a leiomyoma (L) and ovulatory dysfunction from PCOS (O).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>AUB-L and AUB-O</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both categories apply: the submucosal fibroid is a structural cause (PALM — Leiomyoma), and PCOS causes ovulatory dysfunction (COEIN — Ovulatory). Real patients frequently have concurrent structural and non-structural causes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "This patient has two concurrent causes: AUB-L (submucosal leiomyoma — structural, PALM) and AUB-O (ovulatory dysfunction from PCOS — non-structural, COEIN). The PALM-COEIN system allows multiple categories simultaneously, because real patients rarely have a single isolated cause."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. This patient has concurrent causes — a structural leiomyoma (AUB-L) and non-structural ovulatory dysfunction from PCOS (AUB-O). The PALM-COEIN framework allows multiple coexisting categories to be assigned."
                }
              ],
